well back here in germany the german  president frank walter steinmeier today  said that germany needs to take  responsibility for its colonial past he  made the comment at the opening of two  new exhibitions at berlin's humboldt  forum museum complex  the exhibits contain about 20 000  african and asian artifacts and the  origins of some of those objects  are controversial  expressive faces decorated with  thousands of colorful beads adorn the  king's throne from cameroon it's one of  the most spectacular exhibits and one  that shows how difficult it is to handle  objects from the colonial era it was a  gift from the sultan to the kaiser but  how freely given a gift to a colonial  master then there's the large boat from  luff island in what's now papua new  guinea 130 years old 15 meters long  that's without doubt but was it really  bought in 1903 in the colony of german  new guinea there's no evidence it was  many of the 3 000 objects shown here  have an equally unclear history but  clarifying their origins as president  steinmeier said in his opening address  is not the sole task of museums  essentially it's about our self-image  our responsibility before history we and  by that i mean europeans as a whole will  have to overcome established thought  patterns and encourage ourselves to  perceive and accept other perspectives  the humble forum museums are taking on  the task the history of the artifacts is  explained in booklets and online the  museums also want to showcase the great  artistic and cultural significance of  works from africa asia and oceana such  as the 1400 year old cave paintings from  kuka on the silk road which are on  display in a specially designed room  well the opening of the humboldt forum  comes as namibia debates whether or not  to accept a 1 billion euro offer from  germany to compensate for colonial era  atrocities germany is seeking to atone  for its genocide against the herrero and  name people in the first decade of the  last century yesterday 300 protesters  climbed the fences of parliament to  express their anger over the  compensation plan they say the proposal  is a sellout and does not effectively  address the atrocities of german  colonialism the parliamentary debate on  the agreement is expected to be voted on  in the coming days  germany's foreign minister haiku moss  says that germany stands by its offer of  compensation and he says he hopes  namibia will ratify the agreement  we negotiated for a very very long time  over many years and the results that  have been achieved have been achieved in  the spirit of finally arriving at a  result not a conclusion  in this really difficult chapter of  german history  because it's actually just the beginning  of a very very intensive collaboration  between germany and namibia i would like  it to be seen that way in namibia but  ultimately that's a decision that will  be made in namibia  so from possibly looted artifacts to the  horror of colonialism how much should  germany pay and will it ever be enough  for more on this we're joined tonight by  joshua qwesi akins joshua is a political  scientist and a human rights activist  joshua it's good to have you on the  program i mean where do we begin here  this is such a deeply sensitive issue  how can germany right the wrongs of its  colonial past and genocide  will money be enough  well certainly not i mean looking at  namibia it's very clear that three  things are required right there has to  be full recognition of the genocide  there has to be restitution of human  remains because thousands of human  remains skulls that were acquired in the  cosmic genocide are still um here in  germany  and of course there have to be  reparations and unfortunately we just  heard haiku master foreign minister  speak about the fact that he doesn't  want  you know this to be  the final stop but the beginning of a  process unfortunately the german offer  is really structured in such a way that  no further recompense will be possible  after this money is being paid and the  key issue the key criticism here is that  representatives of the groups of  survivors of the nama and the herrera  have not been present in these  negotiations so  the people that were wronged are not  part of the process of writing that  wrong and this clearly shows that  yeah i would say a colonial  continuity can be seen in the way that  the german government refuses to  directly engage with  those whose forebears whose ancestors  have been wronged have been murdered and  have been driven off their ancestral  land in namibia well joshua do we know  why representatives of the herrera and  the nama people have not been included  in in these negotiations i mean if one  would think that they would be the first  people to be invited  that's exactly the issue here um you see  i mean the african continent was divided  right here in berlin right and now the  german government says well  given those borders we can only  negotiate with the namibian government  which of course is a very convenient for  the german government since the libyan  government has very few herrera nama  representatives among them but of course  if you look into german history we  noticed that after the second world war  germany was able to negotiate not just  with the state of israel but with a  plethora of jewish diaspora groups which  was key  in achieving  you know a recompense for  the holocaust and so there's a clear  historical precedent and that's why  people in namibia say it must be  possible for germany to speak with us  directly too  there is also the subject of artifacts  that are believed to have been given to  germany that that's being debated how  easy is it to prove that art and other  valuables were not looted  well this is of course um a tricky issue  however if we look more closely in you  know  the previous video it was clearly stated  that we're talking about ten thousands  of objects the key issue here is not  whether a few of them might have been  gifted or not the key issue is that it's  on record that ten thousands have  directly been looted  there's documentation of that and there  are ten thousands of others where the  circumstances are not clear and so one  of the key criticisms lobbied against  the humboldt forum is that here we have  the biggest cultural project in europe  um 600  million euro were spent on erecting  this museum but  scant resources have been made available  to actually research into the provenians  of the objects that are going to go on  display there and that's a clear  discrepancy and that is why communities  literally from around the world are  saying you cannot put our heritage on  display you must put money into  researching  how this even got to berlin  joshuasi akins we appreciate your time  and your analysis valuable insights  tonight thank you  thank you
 

